 "verdict": "disputed",
 "modern_form": "Two different Greek words: Τάρταρος 'the underworld' (Pre-Greek, Beekes EDG 1455) and Byzantine Greek τάρταρον 'deposit crusting wine casks' (> ML tartarum), the latter probably from Arabic durdī < Persian dord.",
 "note": "The root box itself is a real Greek word with the right gloss — Τάρταρος, the abyss below Hades in Hesiod's Theogony — but it is the wrong root for this tree, and its own etymology is no longer explained as Klein explains it. (a) Modern scholarship (Beekes, Etymological Dictionary of Greek, s.v. Τάρταρος) treats the mythological word as a Pre-Greek substrate word of reduplicated shape; Wiktionary records only Strabo's guess (from Ταρτησσός) and Klein's 'word of imitative origin', both antiquarian. (b) The chemical word is a separate lexeme: Medieval/Byzantine Greek τάρταρον 'tartar of wine casks', which Wiktionary, Skeat, Devic and Funk & Wagnalls all derive from Arabic durdī 'dregs', itself from Persian dord; Etymonline is the dissenting modern voice ('a Semitic source has been proposed ... Arabic is unlikely because of the early date of the word in Latin'). (c) Nourai's arrows therefore run backwards at the top of the tree: Persian dord and Arabic durdī are not descendants of Greek Tartaros; dord is inherited Iranian (Proto-Iranian *dr̥ti-, PIE *dʰreyd- 'to have diarrhoea', a cognate of English dirt) and is the ultimate SOURCE of the European word, if the Arabic etymology is right at all. The lower half of the tree (Arabic/Latin > French tartre > English tartar, plus the modern Arabic/Persian back-loans) is sound. EXTRACTION NOTE: on the printed page the Latin box (#3) hangs from the root's own spine, not from the Arabic box; and the Ferdowsī hemistich is printed «از این آشتی جنگ بهر منست» (آشتی), while the JSON reads آتشی.",
